Docker Networking allows containers to communicate with each other, the host machine, and the outside world. 
When you install Docker, it automatically creates three default networks: Bridge, Host, and None. (There is a category User defined Bridge (Custom))
Another one is MACVLAN (docker swarm), IPVLAN, Overlay

Some commands to view and create a docker network
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~$docker network ls      #to view networks
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~$docker network create mynetwork -d bridge    #making our own network 'mynetwork' of type bridge
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~$docker network ls      #to check the created network

Use two-tier-flask app project/repo 

ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~$cd projects
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects$ git clone two-tier-flask-app.git
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$cd app.py      #read the file to create dockerfile for environment variables

#packages that need to be installed can be understood from developer, application code, README.md file etc

Dockerfile:

# Use an official Python runtime as the base image
FROM python:3.9-slim

# Set the working directory in the container
WORKDIR /app

# install required packages for system
RUN apt-get update \
    && apt-get upgrade -y \
    && apt-get install -y gcc default-libmysqlclient-dev pkg-config \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# Copy the requirements file into the container
COPY requirements.txt .

# Install app dependencies
RUN pip install mysqlclient
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

# Copy the rest of the application code
COPY . .

# Specify the command to run your application
CMD ["python", "app.py"]

To build the app:
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker build -t two-tier-backend .
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker ps      #check the image

Check the app.py file to see the database environment variables as we need mysql image also in the container to run the application. 
We already built the mysql image, so we have it in our images which we will use. Check the port number (5000 in our case, mentioned in last line in app.py file)

ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker images      #check image for our app - two-tier-backend
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker run -d -p 5000:5000 -e MYSQL_HOST=my_sql-e MYSQL_USER=root 
-e MYSQL_PASSWORD=root -e MYSQL_DB=devops two-tier-backend:latest
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker ps -a        #to see all docker images (exited images also)
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker logs         # we check logs with container id which we got using docker ps -a for our image two-tier-backend
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker run -d -p 5000:5000 -e MYSQL_HOST=funny_turing -e MYSQL_USER=root 
-e MYSQL_PASSWORD=root -e MYSQL_DB=devops two-tier-backend:latest

#We are not able to find the image two-tier-backend as it is not there in the network. So lets build a docker network
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker ps              # Stop and remove the conainer id of mysql
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker stop <container_id_for_mysql> && docker rm <container_id_for_mysql>
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker network create two_tier -d bridge      #creates a bridge network with name two_tier

ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker run -d --name mysql --network two-tier -e MY_SQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=root MY_SQL_DATABASE=devops mysql            #this will create mysql database image in our network and
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker run -d -p 5000:5000 --network two-tier -e MYSQL_HOST=mysql -e MYSQL_USER=root 
-e MYSQL_PASSWORD=root -e MYSQL_DB=devops two-tier-backend:latest      #this will create image for two-tier in the network

ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker ps      #check images for both 
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker network ls    #check the networks
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~projects/two-tier-flask-app$docker network inspect two_tier    #check the network

